<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phra Phuttha Chinnarat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · Also known as Phra Buddha Chinnarat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phra Phuttha Chinnarat is a gilded bronze seated Buddha image in Wat Phra Si Rattana Mahathat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, cast in the late Sukhothai style, probably in the fourteenth century. With its flame-shaped halo it is regarded as one of the most beautiful Buddha images in Thailand, and it served as a model for later images, including a copy at Wat Benchamabophit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phra-phuttha-chinnarat.jpg" alt="Phra Phuttha Chinnarat" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ... · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Phra_Si_Rattana_Mahathat,_Phitsanulok_(I).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
